  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 408 AM EDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nRadar imagery this morning shows multiple clusters of storms making \ntheir way across MN, WI, and MI as a shortwave moves through the \nregion. For the UP, the first batch is currently over the central UP \nwhile becoming increasingly disorganized, but not after dropping \nlarge swaths of 0.3-1\" of rain per MRMS (with both more and less in \nsome locations). Driven by a predominantly westerly 300-850 mb mean \nwind and aided by upper-level divergence, this first batch continues \nto move toward cooler air in the low 60s and dewpoints in the high \n50s, leaving the region of higher instability to its south. A few \nstronger cells have popped up, but so far have stayed sub-severe \nwith potential for winds to 30 kts and small hail.\n\nA second round of convection is currently passing Duluth, with parts \nof this line bound east. This long-lived group has evolved over the \npast few hours from multiple tight segments into an elongated line. \nIts path so far has largely followed a NW-SE MUCAPE gradient running \nfrom northern MN toward Green Bay and from there toward lower MI \n(values of 1000 J/kg near MI-WI border and 2000 J/kg in northern WI \nper SPC Mesoanalysis). While southern sections of this line may move \ninto higher instability areas in northern WI and reinvigorate, the \nnorthern portions may detach and head east toward the UP. \nShould this occur, any cells will be moving into cooler, \noverturned air from the previous storms. While it's possible \nthis cooler air will dampen any convection, outflow boundaries \nresiding in the air may also serve to initiate uplift. Given \nproximity of high MUCAPE values and up to 50kts 0-6 km shear, \nwill continue to monitor radar as storms evolve. Though severe \nweather is not expected, a stronger storm or two could occur, \nmost likely near the WI border.\n\nStorms and rain are expected to clear out by late morning, though \nsome low-end precipitation chances (<40%) linger over the western UP \nthrough this evening. Rainfall from this is expected to be less than \na tenth of an inch. Given cloudiness today, high temperatures today \nwill reach into the mid to high 70s, with mostly light, easterly \nflow.\n\nBroad upper level ridging over the central CONUS continues to \ndominate the pattern through the holiday weekend. As such, \ntemperatures will remain above normal into next week with daytime \nhighs residing in the mid to upper 70s to perhaps the low 80s in a \nfew locations each day. The forecast looks to be mostly on the dry \nside through the weekend, but precipitation chances increase again \nearly to mid next week as ridging starts to flatten out and makes \nway for a potentially stronger system to pass through the region. \nDetails are still sparse, but NBM depicts a 50-70% chance for at \nleast a quarter inch of rain across the UP by early Wednesday, and \nalso depicts a 40-70% chance for wind gust of 40 mph or greater over \na wide swath of Lake Superior and the Keweenaw in the same time \nframe. Will continue to monitor this potential as details hopefully \nstart to come into better focus in the coming days.",
